WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.160
Ever since she was in high school, Faith had
dreamed of moving out and finally getting

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:06.240
a place of her own.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:10.920
It wasn't that she didn't love her parents,
she did, but they were always around,

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:16.080
always coming into her room without knocking,
telling her when to go to bed, when to wake

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:18.600
up, not to eat dinner in front of the TV.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.520
In college, she had some welcome independence,
but she had to live with roommates.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:28.380
Again, they were perfectly nice people, some
of them she even considered good friends,

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:33.540
but she hated having to share her space, having
to come home from a long day of classes

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:36.540
and see someone else's dishes piled up in
the sink,

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:39.540
to have someone else's music keeping her awake
at night,

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:43.140
to delay her morning shower because someone
else had gotten there first.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:48.540
So, she was thrilled when, a few years after
graduating, she got a new job at a marketing

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.240
company
that would hopefully let her afford a place

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:52.140
by herself.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:58.440
She anticipated having some peace, some quiet,
and most importantly, some privacy.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:02.220
Faith spent all day moving furniture and unpacking
boxes,

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:05.160
turning the little apartment into a real home.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:09.420
It wasn't much, just a main room she used
as a bedroom and living room,

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:14.040
a bathroom, and a basic little kitchen, but
it was all hers.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:18.840
And all of the little personal touches she'd
put together really made it feel like the

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:19.920
place was meant for her.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:24.540
There was the orange couch she'd picked up
at the vintage store downtown, the coffee

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:26.940
table she'd gotten for free from her previous
roommate,

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:32.220
framed pictures of her family and friends,
a bed with linens in her favorite color, light

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:35.520
blue,
and lots and lots of potted plants.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:41.520
Over time, she would find even more things,
more little personal touches to liven up the

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:41.760
space.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.640
Now, how to celebrate her new home?

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:50.460
Her gas wasn't on yet, so she couldn't cook
anything, but that was the perfect excuse

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:51.720
to order in!

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:56.820
Thirty minutes or less later, she was kicking
back on her couch with a large cheese pizza,

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:59.340
watching a scary movie on her TV.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:01.920
No one would ask to switch it over to The
Bachelor,

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.680
no one would take the last slice while she
was in the bathroom.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:06.840
It was perfect.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:11.760
A little piece of heaven, an introvert's paradise.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.720
As she excitedly devouring her first meal
in the new place, she began to feel her eyes

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.620
grow heavy,
the day of strenuous unpacking catching up

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:20.220
with her body.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.860
Just as she was thinking about calling it
a night,

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:27.960
a pale-faced monster appeared out of nowhere
onscreen, terrifying the movie's main character,

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:32.100
and making Faith jump, spilling her soda all
over the coffee table.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:33.120
"Dammit!"

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:34.200
She groaned.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:35.760
Already, she'd made a mess.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:38.280
But hey, she tried to look on the bright side.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:41.400
It was yet another first&nbsp;
to break in the apartment.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:46.380
She grabbed a kitchen towel to mop up the
soda, and when she returned to the main room,

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:47.760
she froze.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.380
There was something in the window, standing
out against the darkness outside.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:56.460
At first, she thought it was a reflection,
a trick of the light,

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:02.160
but as she slowly approached the window, eyes
wide and hands trembling, she got a better

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:02.400
look.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:07.800
There was a face there, a little difficult
to make out, as if it were peering out of

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:10.380
the shadows, but unmistakable nonetheless.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:16.860
It was pale, human, vaguely resembling a strange
man with dark circles under his eyes,

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.260
and he was looking right at her.

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:22.920
She didn't scream, she was too terrified to
make a sound.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:27.900
She stared at the face for a long moment,
waiting for it to do something, anything.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:33.060
After several moments of the tensest staring
contest of her life, she blinked.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:34.680
The face didn't budge.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:38.880
"You need to leave," she eventually spoke,
surprising herself as she did.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:40.980
"If you don't get out of here, I'm calling
the cops."

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:42.780
Again, the face did nothing.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:43.980
"Do you hear me?!"

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:48.180
her voice climbed in pitch, her heart pounding
against the inside of her chest.

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:49.200
Who was he?

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:50.760
What did he want?

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:54.840
She became vaguely aware of the fact that
she couldn't see the rest of the man's body.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:58.860
Just his face, looking at her with a neutral
expression,

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:04.320
a look of vague curiosity like someone watching
a caged animal at the zoo might have.

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:07.860
It was then that she remembered something
that made her blood run cold,

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:11.100
made the towel drop from her hands as her
stomach sank:

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.440
she lived on the third floor.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:17.880
Her apartment had no balcony, no fire escape
he could have climbed up.

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:22.980
Whatever was looking at her through her window,
it was no ordinary peeping tom.

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:28.320
She couldn't say how long she stared at the
face, its unblinking eyes, its inscrutable

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:29.040
expression.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:34.620
She moved closer to the window, step by step,
until she was almost nose to nose with the

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:34.800
thing.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:38.220
She couldn't think of it as a person, though
it looked like one.

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:39.180
Mostly.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:44.400
She should run screaming out the door, looking
for some kind of help, but what would she

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:44.700
say?

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:48.000
That there was a floating face looking at
her through the window?

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:50.640
What if it was somehow all in her head?

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:53.760
Some sort of hallucination&nbsp;
brought on by exhaustion&nbsp;

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:55.440
and the stress of the move?

00:04:55.440 --> 00:05:00.300
She raised a fist and, following a passing
instinct, rapped on the glass.

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:05.700
\She didn't know what she expected, for it
to blink, to move, to knock back?

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:06.780
To say something?

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:08.100
But nothing happened.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:12.000
It just stayed there, as if it were part of
the window itself.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.600
Faith pulled the curtains closed, hiding the
strange face from view.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:17.880
With any luck, it would be gone in the morning.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:22.800
She had worked hard to get this apartment,
and she would be damned if she let some strange

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:26.460
thing, whatever it was, drive her out of her
new home.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:31.140
But as she climbed into bed and pulled the
covers up to her chin, she could still feel

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:34.620
its gaze on her,
as if it could see through the thick fabric

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:35.460
of the curtains.

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:40.320
She rolled over onto her side, her back to
the window, and tried her best to forget about

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.500
it,
to shake that horrible feeling of being observed.

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:47.640
She lay there for hours, eyes open, heart
racing.

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:51.780
But finally, the exhaustion won and she slipped
away into sleep.

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:56.280
She didn't dream at all that night, it was
as if she closed her eyes, and seconds later,

00:05:56.280 --> 00:05:57.180
it was morning.

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:01.380
She woke to the beep of her alarm, feeling
as if she hadn't rested at all.

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:04.860
The first thing she did was climb out of bed,
and go to the window.

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:08.340
Carefully, tentatively, she opened the curtains.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:10.380
She let out a sigh of deep relief.

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:11.940
The face was gone.

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:14.160
She must have imagined it after all.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:19.080
A bit concerning, but as long as it didn't
happen again, she wouldn't have anything to

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:19.500
worry about.

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:22.800
Lack of sleep does strange things to the mind,
after all.

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:27.780
No longer plagued by the fear of the strange
face, Faith hopped into the shower and let

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:31.920
the worries of the previous night wash away,
disappearing down the drain.

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:35.880
She brushed her teeth, dried her hair, did
her makeup, and dressed for work.

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:39.480
After a banana and a quick cup of coffee,
she was out the door.

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:44.580
Work was exceptionally busy that day, and
it pushed any memory of the oddity in the

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:46.440
window out of her mind entirely.

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:51.000
She was even feeling good enough to accept
her friend's offer to grab a drink after work.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:55.920
They met up at a bar down the street from
her new place, sharing cocktails, memories,

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:57.540
and a plate of onion rings.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:00.060
It was a perfect, lovely evening.

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:05.040
By the time she got home, savoring the click
of the key in the lock as she let herself

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:09.480
into her quiet little sanctuary,
she had completely forgotten about the unusual

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:10.140
apparition.

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:15.180
She swung the door open and rushed to dump
herself onto the couch for a bit of TV before

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:15.480
bed.

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:19.680
Then, out of the corner of her eye, she saw
it.

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:24.780
The face, back in its previous place, staring
at her from the darkness outside.

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:28.440
She gasped at the sight, her scream catching
in her throat.

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:32.580
It was just as she remembered it- wait, no,
something was different.

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:33.660
What was it...?

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:37.860
She clapped a hand over her mouth in horror
as she identified the change: its expression

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:38.400
was different.

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:41.640
Gone was the vaguely&nbsp;
interested, neutral expression.

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:44.220
Now it was smiling at her.

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:47.880
Not a pleasant smile, not a warm, friendly
smile.

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:51.120
A wicked smile, twisted and cruel.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:57.960
It knew she was afraid, that she'd been hoping
it was gone forever, and it was enjoying itself.

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:01.440
Her hands were shaking, nervous sweat beading
on her brow.

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:02.820
"What do you want from me?"

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:04.740
She whispered, staring into its eyes.

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:09.000
They didn't move, but they glittered with
malicious glee just the same.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:10.440
"What do you want from me?!"

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:13.380
She repeated, her voice rising to a shout.

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:14.160
"Shut up!"

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:16.560
Her next-door neighbor yelled, banging on
the wall.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:17.880
"It's after midnight!"

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:22.200
She couldn't bring herself to respond, she
had more important things to attend to.

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:25.320
Her stomach turned, bile rising in her throat.

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:27.240
Would the face be gone again in the morning?

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:29.340
Would it come back again at night?

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:34.680
Was the apartment haunted, possessed by a
specter doomed to reside in her window forever?

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:37.140
Was that why the rent had been so affordable?

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:41.580
Or maybe it was her, maybe this thing had
been drawn to her?

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:47.880
Or maybe she was losing her mind, unaccustomed
to living alone, and her sanity was slipping

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:48.060
away.

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:49.800
She shivered at the thought.

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:54.300
What was worse: if the face was all in her
head...or if it wasn't?

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:58.740
Rather than attempt to answer the question
gnawing at her, she closed the curtains.

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:01.260
Out of sight, out of mind, she thought.

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:04.140
She would just have to keep the curtains closed,
possibly forever.

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:08.220
It was better than seeing that damned face
grinning that horrible grin.

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:13.140
But as she tried again to settle in for another
restless night, she could feel its eyes boring

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:15.780
into her, burning into her skull.

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:18.420
She had never felt so uneasy before.

00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:24.240
She'd prepared for cockroaches, for black
mold, for burglars, but not for this.

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:29.520
This was something outside of her understanding,
outside of reality itself.

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:30.840
"Just don't look."

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:32.280
She whispered to herself.

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:33.660
"Just don't look at it."

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:38.760
It became part of her daily routine: wake
up, ignore the window, go to work, try to

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:42.180
forget about the window,
get home, try desperately not to think about

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:42.600
the window.

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:44.700
She could always feel it, though.

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:49.740
As soon as the sun went down, though she kept
the curtains closed, she knew it was there.

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:54.420
She thought about checking to see what face
it was making this time, but she couldn't

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:55.620
bring herself to look.

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:57.780
Gradually, her condition worsened.

00:09:57.780 --> 00:10:02.940
She woke to a tightness in her chest, panic
attacks that wracked her body, and felt like

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:04.440
her heart might stop beating.

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:07.620
Her stomach would ache, she struggled to keep
food down.

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:11.040
One morning she rushed to the bathroom, and
spat up blood.

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:16.380
The effect began to follow her out of the
apartment, too, far away from the face's dwelling.

00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:20.640
She would sit at her desk, struggling to focus
on marketing campaigns and pitches,

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:25.440
and she would feel a sudden pain in her stomach,
forcing her to double over and groan with

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:25.860
agony.

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:29.940
She would see flickers of the pale face in
her peripheral vision, turning to look,

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:32.040
only to find that there was nothing there.

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:36.000
When she walked down the hall to the elevator,
or down the street outside her building,

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:39.000
she would feel as if someone was following
her.

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:43.260
She never saw anyone, never heard any footsteps
coming up behind her,

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:45.960
but she could never shake off the sensation.

00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:51.480
Soon, even her formerly dreamless nights of
sleep, as fitful as they had been, became

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:51.960
unsafe.

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:55.020
She would toss and turn, sweating through
her sheets,

00:10:55.020 --> 00:10:59.700
as her subconscious was tormented by chilling
visions and terrible nightmares.

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:04.500
She would dream of walking down a long, dark
hallway that seemed to go on forever,

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:08.880
and something coming up behind her, trying
to grab her and drag her away.

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:13.920
It would chase her, wide dark eyes and long,
long limbs grasping at her,

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:16.560
as she ran and ran but never escaped.

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:19.440
Other times she would dream of being locked
in a damp basement,

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:23.700
chained to a wall as the pale creature sat
in a chair across from her,

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:24.960
just watching.

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:28.500
Waiting for something, though she didn't know
what.

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:32.880
Every night, another nightmare that made her
wake up screaming, fighting off invisible

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:33.420
attackers.

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:37.500
After two weeks of living in hell, she decided
to look out the window again.

00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:40.560
She had to see, had to know.

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:43.680
That night, when she opened the curtains,
it was there.

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:46.140
Mouth wide open, eyes glinting.

00:11:46.140 --> 00:11:47.580
It was laughing at her.

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:49.680
Screw the deposit, enough was enough.

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:51.420
She wanted her home back.

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:55.740
She picked up the heavy lamp on her bedside
table, wound up, and threw it at the window

00:11:55.740 --> 00:11:56.580
with all her might.

00:11:56.580 --> 00:12:01.380
The glass shattered, little crystalline shards
spraying in every direction as the lamp flew

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:04.200
through the air, out the window, and down
to the street below.

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:07.200
And just like that, the face was gone.

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:10.500
She checked every other window in the place,
the little one in the bathroom,

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:14.280
the peephole on her door, but it was nowhere
to be found.

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:15.600
She was free.

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:20.400
Her knees gave out, and she collapsed to the
floor, her face in her hands, and wept from

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:21.180
sheer relief.

00:12:21.180 --> 00:12:25.380
Tomorrow she would clean up the broken glass
and spin her landlord a story about a freak

00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:25.620
accident.

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:30.360
Someone would come to fix the window, and
it would be the best money she had ever spent.

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:34.020
Eventually, the whole ordeal would fade from
her memory like a bad dream,

00:12:34.020 --> 00:12:38.880
but somewhere out there, someone else was
living the nightmare all over again,

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:46.380
looking out a darkened window to see an unwelcome
guest: the entity known as SCP-965.

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:50.820
SCP-965 is a phenomenon affecting framed windows.

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:56.040
When it appears, it manifests in the shape
of a shadowy face, belonging to a pale man

00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:57.480
staring in through the window.

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:00.540
The details of the man's face vary from person
to person,

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:04.020
as well as his apparent age and the direction
he is facing.

00:13:04.020 --> 00:13:07.620
However, all reports point to the same figure
at various ages,

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:10.140
ranging from 10 to 55.

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:14.400
The Foundation has attempted to use facial
recognition software to identify a citizen

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:19.080
matching the description of this figure,
but so far, no one has been found.

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:22.560
SCP-965 will not appear in just any window.

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:27.360
It will only manifest when the lighting on
the outside of the window drops below a lumination

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:28.740
of five candelas.

00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:32.640
The lighting on the inside of the window does
not have any measurable effect.

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:37.500
The face will only appear in the confines
of a completely assembled window frame,

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:41.160
though the window does not necessarily need
to be installed anywhere.

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:46.380
It will only move from one glass pane to another
if its original point of manifestation is

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:46.980
destroyed.

00:13:46.980 --> 00:13:51.960
The face can be seen from an outside vantage
point, though it has been described by observers

00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:54.120
as "looking away, into the room."

00:13:54.120 --> 00:14:00.480
When SCP-965 first appears to someone, it
produces feelings of unease, anxiety, and

00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:01.740
low-grade paranoia.

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:05.880
Anyone within the visual range of the affected
window will experience these feelings,

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:09.360
even if the window is covered by curtains
or any other means.

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:15.720
Any individual that sleeps in an area visible
to SCP-965's manifestation point will begin

00:14:15.720 --> 00:14:20.580
to experience difficulty sleeping,
suffering from upsetting and disturbing dreams.

00:14:20.580 --> 00:14:25.080
However, this is not the endpoint of the entity's
impact on those it appears to.

00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:29.100
At a point between three and ten nights of
sleep after its initial appearance,

00:14:29.100 --> 00:14:33.360
the entity's facial expression will begin
to shift into a noticeable smile.

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:38.280
After this shift, the victim's symptoms will
become physical as well as mental,

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:42.780
including ulcers and intestinal bleeding,
heartburn, abdominal pain,

00:14:42.780 --> 00:14:44.520
and even vomiting blood.

00:14:44.520 --> 00:14:49.260
This has been attributed to the entity's influence
on the human body's reaction to heightened

00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:50.880
levels of stress and fear.

00:14:50.880 --> 00:14:56.640
The subjects that reach this stage of exposure
to SCP-965 begin to see its face in windows

00:14:56.640 --> 00:14:59.760
in their dreams,
as well as spotting it in their peripheral

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:02.160
vision while going about their waking life.

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:06.480
They begin to see the face out of the corner
of their eye even when the affected window

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:07.860
is nowhere in sight.

00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:12.660
These additional sightings are accompanied
by lingering feelings of paranoia and the

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:15.960
sensation that something is following or watching
them.

00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:20.280
Though the entity has never made a sound,
and does not move while it is visible,

00:15:20.280 --> 00:15:23.700
it can disappear and reappear in different
poses.

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:28.980
It has also displayed notable signs of sentience,
appearing disappointed when it manifests in

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:32.760
an empty room,
and angry if it sees someone who broke its

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:33.360
previous window.

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:37.140
When presented with one of the agents who
first brought it into custody,

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:40.080
the entity appeared frightened for the first
time.

00:15:40.080 --> 00:15:44.160
Testing involving SCP-965&nbsp;
involving the destruction&nbsp;

00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:45.240
of its host window

00:15:45.240 --> 00:15:50.040
confirmed that a multi-paned window might
act as multiple holding zones,

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:54.240
but significant damage to its overall structure
keeps it from being a viable replacement.

00:15:54.240 --> 00:16:00.540
In this particular case, the entity manifested
in a nearby experimentation chamber's observation

00:16:00.540 --> 00:16:03.540
window,
which was promptly destroyed in order to prevent

00:16:03.540 --> 00:16:04.920
any potential breaches.

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:09.540
For the month following this incident, the
entity manifested with noticeably hostile

00:16:09.540 --> 00:16:12.720
facial expressions,
clearly resentful of its treatment.

00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:19.020
Only one other notable incident has occurred
so far during the course of SCP-965's containment

00:16:19.020 --> 00:16:20.580
by the SCP Foundation.

00:16:20.580 --> 00:16:24.360
Dr. L - the rest of her name has been redacted
from the official file

00:16:24.360 --> 00:16:28.500
- was the head researcher assigned to SCP-965
for several months

00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:32.760
before she filed an official request for transfer
to a different test subject.

00:16:32.760 --> 00:16:38.160
She was beginning to experience intrusive
visions of SCP-965 and lingering feelings

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:38.940
of paranoia,

00:16:38.940 --> 00:16:42.060
lasting long after she left the Foundation
Site.

00:16:42.060 --> 00:16:46.200
Her symptoms were consistent with those of
someone who had slept in the presence of the

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:48.960
entity,
though she swore up and down she had never

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:52.500
napped or slept at all in the vicinity of
the affected window.

00:16:52.500 --> 00:16:57.120
She was temporarily relieved of her duties
and provided with psychological care.

00:16:57.120 --> 00:17:03.720
So far, no other instances of SCP-965 impacting
staff who have not slept in its presence have

00:17:03.720 --> 00:17:07.200
been reported,
but this case set an unsettling precedent.

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:13.560
The mental health of anyone assigned to SCP-965
is to be strictly monitored, in case it expands

00:17:13.560 --> 00:17:15.060
its influence again.

00:17:15.060 --> 00:17:18.240
SCP-965 is contained within a framed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:19.320
ready-to-install
window,

00:17:19.320 --> 00:17:23.760
made up of six panes of clear glass, or other
comparable material,

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:25.500
at a size of at least 15cmx30cm.

00:17:27.420 --> 00:17:31.440
The window must be kept in an environmentally
controlled storage facility

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:35.160
capable of withstanding earthquakes and other
seismic activity.

00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:39.600
The window must be inspected at least once
a week in order to check the integrity of

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:40.200
the material.

00:17:40.200 --> 00:17:44.580
Additionally, at least two identical framed
windows must be stored in the same facility,

00:17:44.580 --> 00:17:47.640
in separate chambers with additional insulation.

00:17:47.640 --> 00:17:53.160
Any lighting in the containment chamber should
be kept at a minimum of 130 candelas at any

00:17:53.160 --> 00:17:54.600
time personnel are inside,&nbsp;

00:17:54.600 --> 00:17:57.900
with the exception of&nbsp;
research and experimentation.

00:17:57.900 --> 00:18:04.140
Though SCP-965 is currently contained, the
Foundation is unable to control its movement

00:18:04.140 --> 00:18:06.240
should its current window be destroyed.

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:10.320
Therefore, SCP-965 is classified as "Euclid."

00:18:10.320 --> 00:18:14.340
Scopophobia is the fear of being watched or
looked at by others.

00:18:14.340 --> 00:18:17.460
Those suffering from this fear will often
avoid windows,

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:20.220
terrified of who might be standing on the
other side,

00:18:20.220 --> 00:18:23.520
staring in, and keeping track of everything
they do.

00:18:23.520 --> 00:18:28.140
Even those of us without scopophobia might
find ourselves feeling a prickle of dread

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:32.520
while looking out the window at night,
watching for a shadowy figure or a ghastly

00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:33.900
face pressed to the glass.

00:18:33.900 --> 00:18:36.600
Most of the time, of course, there is no one
there.

00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:41.640
It's just an overactive imagination,
the lingering effect of watching one too many

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:42.420
scary movies.

00:18:42.420 --> 00:18:45.540
But eventually, your luck might just run out.

00:18:45.540 --> 00:18:50.220
One night, when the world falls quiet, and
you go to close the curtains before you go

00:18:50.220 --> 00:18:54.900
to sleep, just in case…
you might just find that someone is right

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:57.960
there,
on the other side of that thin pane of glass,

00:18:57.960 --> 00:19:01.560
staring at you with wide, unblinking eyes.

00:19:01.560 --> 00:19:05.940
It won't ever come inside, but it isn't going
anywhere,

00:19:05.940 --> 00:19:08.760
and as long as you are where it can see you?

00:19:08.760 --> 00:19:11.700
You will never know peace again.

00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:19.860
Now go check out “SCP-1471 - MalO Version
1.0.0” and “SCP-966 - Sleep Killer”

00:19:19.860 --> 00:19:24.000
for more relentless anomalies that’ll seriously
interfere with your sleep schedule…

